+\textbf{Infernal.} Only 457 of Rfam 15.1's 4{,}227 families (10.8\%) changed. Before the check accounted for record length, removing one family took 666\,s against a 328\,s stock run: exact, but $0.49\times$, and the cache fill took $5.2\times$ a stock run. A bacterial genome is one sequence or a handful, so the audit and the random pads covered the whole genome, and that audit took as long as a stock run. The shipped check now skips the audit and the random-pad sample when any one record is longer than half the residues. The neighbor pad and the in-situ check still run. A skipped layer misses a dependency the neighbor pad does not cover; on the pilot before this change, the random pads' miss probability was 0.86.
